Create hello.cpp:
#include <iostream>
int main() {
std::cout << "Hello, World!" << std::endl;
return 0;
}

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
int main() {
std::string name;
std::cout << "What is your name? ";
std::getline(std::cin, name);
std::cout << "Hi, " << name << "!\n";
return 0;
}
New pieces:
#include <string> — pulls in std::string.
std::getline(std::cin, name) — reads a whole line from standard input.
Stream operator << chains naturally.

Bigger projects split declarations into headers (.h) and implementations into source files (.cpp).
greeter.h
#pragma once
#include <string>
std::string greet(const std::string& name);
greeter.cpp
#include "greeter.h"
std::string greet(const std::string& name) {
return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}
main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "greeter.h"
int main() {
std::cout << greet("Ada") << "\n";
return 0;
}
Compile both .cpp files:
g++ -std=c++20 -Wall greeter.cpp main.cpp -o app
The compiler builds each .cpp into an object file, then the linker combines them into one executable.

Write echo.cpp that reads a line of input and prints it back uppercased.
Hint: #include <algorithm> and std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(), ::toupper);.
Solution
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
int main() {
std::string s;
std::getline(std::cin, s);
std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(),
[](unsigned char c){ return std::toupper(c); });
std::cout << s << "\n";
}

Read two integers from input, print their sum.
Solution
#include <iostream>
int main() {
int a, b;
std::cin >> a >> b;
std::cout << a + b << "\n";
}
